CURRENT PROBLEM
title: Cheapest Route Through Toll Roads (#9901); difficulty: medium
family: graphs
tags: graph, shortest-path, dijkstra, heap-priority-queue
statement:
<<<DATA statement
You are given n towns numbered 0 to n-1 and a list of toll roads roads[i] = [a, b, toll] connecting town a and town b in both directions for the given toll. Return the smallest total toll to travel from town src to town dst, or -1 if no route exists.
>>>
constraints:
<<<DATA constraints
- 1 <= n <= 10^4
- 0 <= roads.length <= 10^5
- 1 <= toll <= 10^6
- src != dst
>>>
leetcode_hints (rewrite, never quote):
<<<DATA leetcode_hints
1. Think of every town as a node and every toll road as a weighted edge between two nodes.
2. Always expand the town with the smallest known total toll first, then update its neighbours.
>>>

ANCHORS (problems this student already solved; cite by title only)
1. Minimum Cost Courier Route (medium)
   why: same idea: best-first search with a priority queue; solved on 2026-04-08; attempts to AC: 1
   student's accepted code (excerpt, first 40 of 45 lines; data):
<<<DATA anchor_code minimum-cost-courier-route
class Solution {
public:
    // synthetic fixture: not real student code
    int courier(int n, vector<vector<int>>& roads, int src, int dst) {
        vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> adj(n);
        for (auto& r : roads) {
            adj[r[0]].push_back({r[1], r[2]});
            adj[r[1]].push_back({r[0], r[2]});
        }
        vector<long long> best(n, LLONG_MAX);
        priority_queue<pair<long long,int>, vector<pair<long long,int>>, greater<>> pq;
        best[src] = 0;
        pq.push({0, src});
        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [d, u] = pq.top(); pq.pop();
            if (d > best[u]) continue;
            for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
                if (best[u] + w < best[v]) {
                    best[v] = best[u] + w;
                    pq.push({best[v], v});
                }
            }
        }
        return best[dst] == LLONG_MAX ? -1 : (int)best[dst];
    }
};
